    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, thanks. Could you expand a bit more on the kinetics of the reaction? I'm specifically interested in why the onset of the irritation is so sudden and seemingly 'aggressive' once the cell walls are breached. It feels almost like a defensive mechanism that happens near-instantaneously.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The rapid production of propanethial S-oxide is indeed instantaneous, and its swift onset is due to the intracellular release of the enzyme alliinase from the sliced onion cells. This sudden escape of alliinase into the surrounding environment leads to the immediate generation of propanethial S-oxide, which reacts tightly with water to produce the irritating compounds. The extremely short latency period - often reported to be as short as 15 seconds to 2 minutes, depending on factors such as the variety, freshness, and moisture content of the onion - contributes to the sudden onset of the stinging sensation.",
      "source": "target",
